update public.moo_versions set body_md = body_md || E'| Collect and organize information (Step 1) | Acquire authoritative hazard and climate data; document baseline, scenarios, time horizons, resolution, metadata and limitations; identify data gaps.                | Hazard/climate inventory and data-gap register             |\n| Scope potential impacts (Step 2)          | For each hazard/stressor, identify potential impacts by sector and element at risk; construct impact chains; validate with sector offices and communities.           | Hazard-impact scoping matrix                               |\n| Develop exposure database (Step 3)        | Build georeferenced exposure units; standardize IDs and attributes; validate spatial joins, completeness, reference dates and privacy controls.                      | Exposure geodatabase and data dictionary                   |\n| Conduct risk assessment (Step 4)          | Apply current DHSUD prescribed vulnerability and risk methods, parameters and tools; retain calculations, assumptions and uncertainty; have sector experts validate. | Risk tables, maps, calculation files and validation record |\n| Summarize findings and MDAs (Step 5)      | Synthesize risk results into Major Decision Areas and policy interventions; identify affected places, groups, systems and responsible planning decisions.            | MDA register and policy-intervention matrix                |\n| Compile results (Step 6)                  | Prepare report, map atlas, geodatabase, technical annexes, data sources, consultation records and executive summary; correct inconsistencies before sign-off.        | Complete CDRA package                                      |\n| Mainstream into CLUP (Step 7)             | CPDO translates MDAs into spatial policies, land-use suitability, overlays, zoning and development control proposals through formal planning process.                | CDRA-to-CLUP crosswalk and proposed amendments             |\n| Monitor and evaluate (Step 8)             | Assign indicators, data owners and review frequency; update after material hazard, exposure, vulnerability, climate or land-use change.                              | CDRA M&E matrix and update log                             |\n| Communicate (Step 9)                      | Prepare accessible, audience-specific risk communication and explain uncertainty, implications and action choices.                                                   | CDRA communication products and feedback record            |\n\n## SOP 3-4 \\| Critical Facilities and Dependency/Failure-Chain Analysis\n\n**Figure 3.14**\n\n*SOP 3-4 Process Flow: Critical Facilities and Dependency/Failure-Chain Analysis*\n\n![Figure 14: SOP 3-4 | Critical Facilities and Dependency/Failure-Chain Analysis](/library/ch3v10/fig-14.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n**Process** **owner**: CDRRMO coordinates; asset owners are accountable for asset data. Purpose: Identify critical service dependencies and inform protection and restoration priorities.\n\n**Table 3.6**\n\n*SOP 3-4 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                   |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-04 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                             |\n| Trigger           | Yearly; after a change to a critical asset; before a contingency plan or PSCP update        |\n| Forms             | Form 3-05 Critical Facilities and Dependency Register                                       |\n| IAT indicators    | A5.8                                                                                        |\n| Policy references | RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule I Sec. 5(b),(d); UNDRR Scorecard 2.3.1                             |\n| Records kept in   | Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register; failure-chain sheets                            |\n\n| **Step / control point** | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                         | **Mandatory record / output**              |\n|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| Define asset scope       | Confirm critical services and asset categories with department heads, utilities and NGAs; assign asset owner and unique ID.                                   | Approved asset scope and owner list        |\n| Inventory assets         | Record coordinates, function, capacity, condition, hazard exposure, service population, operator and alternate arrangements.                                  | Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register |\n| Map dependencies         | Identify required inputs, upstream services, downstream users, access routes, communications, fuel, staff, ICT and utilities.                                 | Dependency matrix and network diagram      |\n| Develop failure chains   | For priority scenarios, trace initiating hazard through asset failure to service disruption and population consequence; mark unverified links as assumptions. | Failure-chain scenario sheets              |\n| Validate with operators  | Conduct structured validation with asset owners and service providers; correct errors and document unknowns.                                                  | Signed validation records                  |\n| Prioritize treatments    | Apply approved criteria to consequence, likelihood, criticality, redundancy, recovery time and vulnerable-group effects; document rationale.                  | Protection/restoration priority register   |\n| Integrate and monitor    | Transfer actions to PSCP, contingency plans, asset plans and AIP/LDIP; monitor completion and service resilience.                                             | Action register and monitoring report      |\n\n## SOP 3-5 \\| LDRRMP and DRR/CCA Mainstreaming Interface\n\n**Figure 3.15**\n\n*SOP 3-5 Process Flow: LDRRMP and DRR/CCA Mainstreaming Interface*\n\n![Figure 15: SOP 3-5 | LDRRMP and DRR/CCA Mainstreaming Interface](/library/ch3v10/fig-15.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n**Process** **owner**: CDRRMO coordinates LDRRMP; CPDO leads development-plan integration. Purpose: Ensure risk evidence is converted into plans, programmes and budget decisions.\n\n**Table 3.7**\n\n*SOP 3-5 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                   |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-05 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                             |\n| Trigger           | Yearly planning and budget cycle (AIP, LDRRMFIP); LDRRMP review; each investment proposal   |\n| Forms             | Form 3-09 DRR/CCA Project Screening; Form 3-04 Major Decision Area Register                 |\n| IAT indicators    | A3.1, A3.2, C1.1, C1.2, C1.4                                                                |\n| Policy references | RA 10121 Sec. 2 and 12(c)(6); RA 7160; RA 9729 as amended by RA 10174; JMC 2013-1           |\n| Records kept in   | Screening records; LDC and LFC minutes; CDRA-to-CLUP/CDP crosswalk                          |\n\n| **Step / control point**                | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                         | **Mandatory record / output**                           |\n|-----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\n| Compile planning evidence               | CDRRMO provides approved CDRA, maps, MDA register, contingency findings, losses, ecosystem and dependency analysis.                           | Planning evidence pack                                  |\n| Convene planning review                 | CPDO convenes LDC and sector offices; review existing CLUP/CDP/LCCAP/ELA/LDIP/AIP and identify policy/programme gaps.                         | Planning review minutes and gap matrix                  |\n| Set objectives and interventions        | Translate risk findings into prevention, mitigation, preparedness and adaptation outcomes; assign measurable outputs and responsible offices. | Results framework and intervention matrix               |\n| Screen spatial and investment proposals | Apply DRR/CCA screening to proposed sites, designs and service models; route technical/regulatory questions to competent offices.             | Completed screening form and technical comments         |\n| Prioritize and resource                 | CPDO/LDC and Budget/LFC use applicable planning and budget processes to prioritize viable, costed interventions.                              | Prioritized project list and budget records             |\n| Approve, implement and monitor          | Submit plans and proposals through statutory approval routes; track outputs, outcomes, risk reduction and corrective action.                  | Approved plans, implementation reports and M&E register |\n\n## SOP 3-6 \\| Hazard-Specific Contingency Plan Development and Control\n\n**Figure 3.16**\n\n*SOP 3-6 Process Flow: Hazard-Specific Contingency Plan Development and Control*\n\n![Figure 16: SOP 3-6 | Hazard-Specific Contingency Plan Development and Control](/library/ch3v10/fig-16.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n**Process** **owner**: CDRRMO as coordinating process owner; hazard/sector leads as technical owners. Purpose: Prepare and maintain tested plans for priority hazard scenarios.\n\n**Table 3.8**\n\n*SOP 3-6 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                                     |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow                   |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-06 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                                               |\n| Trigger           | A priority hazard without a current plan; a plan past its validity; after an exercise or event; yearly review |\n| Forms             | Form 3-06 Contingency Plan Quality and Exercise Record; Form 3-11                                             |\n| IAT indicators    | A3.3, A3.6                                                                                                    |\n| Policy references | RA 10121 Sec. 12(c); NDRRMC–OCD Contingency Planning Guidebook (2020)                                         |\n| Records kept in   | Approved plan; exercise report; corrective-action register (MODE contingency-plan register)                   |\n\n| **Step / control point**                       | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                                                 | **Mandatory record / output**                          |\n|------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|\n| Select priority scenario                       | Use current CDRA, authoritative hazard information, historical impacts and sector risk; record why the hazard/scenario is prioritized.                                                | Scenario selection note                                |\n| Confirm planning team and source guidance      | Designate lead/support offices, barangay and community participants; use the NDRRMC–OCD Contingency Planning Guidebook (2020), its CP Forms 1 to 11, and applicable sector protocols. | Team designation and source register                   |\n| Build scenario and assumptions                 | Define plausible scenario, location, magnitude, timing, warning lead time, exposed population, cascading impacts and uncertainty using authoritative evidence.                        | Scenario and assumptions sheet                         |\n| Set objectives and concept of operations       | Define operational priorities, phases, coordination, field command/EOC interface, escalation and transition to recovery.                                                              | Concept of operations                                  |\n| Assign tasks and resources                     | Specify lead/support agency, task, trigger, resource, communication, reporting and request route; validate contacts and capacity.                                                     | Task matrix, resource gap and contact registers        |\n| Complete protection and inclusion arrangements | Set warning accessibility, evacuation, shelter/CCCM, child protection, health, MHPSS, disability access and environmental safeguards.                                                 | Protection/inclusion checklist and operational annexes |\n| Validate and approve                           | Conduct technical workshop/tabletop; resolve contradictions; obtain approval through the City route established by current issuance.                                                  | Validation minutes and approved plan                   |\n| Exercise and correct                           | Conduct scheduled exercise; evaluate objectives and decision points; assign corrective action owner and deadline; update plan version.                                                | Exercise report and corrective-action register         |\n| Review and maintain                            | Review after exercise, event, material change in hazard data, staffing, resource or contact arrangements and at the approved periodic interval.                                       | Annual review and revision history                     |\n\n## SOP 3-7 \\| Public Service Continuity Planning\n\n**Figure 3.17**\n\n*SOP 3-7 Process Flow: Public Service Continuity Planning*\n\n![Figure 17: SOP 3-7 | Public Service Continuity Planning](/library/ch3v10/fig-17.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n**Process** **owner**: City continuity executive to be designated; department heads own service continuity. Purpose: Protect essential City functions during disruption.\n\n**Table 3.9**\n\n*SOP 3-7 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                   |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-07 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                             |\n| Trigger           | Mayor''s directive; yearly review; after a disruption or exercise                            |\n| Forms             | Form 3-07 Essential Functions Inventory and BIA; Form 3-11                                  |\n| IAT indicators    | A3.4, B2.7                                                                                  |\n| Policy references | NDRRMC–OCD PSCP Guidebook                                                                   |\n| Records kept in   | Approved PSCP; BIA worksheets; exercise report                                              |\n\n| **Step / control point**         | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                               | **Mandatory record / output**                 |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| Establish governance             | Mayor/City Administrator designates continuity executive, coordinators, plan custodian, activation authority and approval route.                                    | Designation/authority record                  |\n| Inventory essential functions    | Each department identifies services, legal basis, service users, minimum service level, process owner, staff, records, facilities, ICT and suppliers.               | Essential Functions Inventory                 |\n| Conduct BIA                      | Assess disruption impacts over time, statutory deadlines, life-safety and welfare consequences, dependencies and tolerable disruption; justify recovery priorities. | BIA worksheet and validated priority schedule |\n| Analyze dependencies and risks   | Map staff, power, water, telecom, ICT, facilities, transport, records, suppliers, fuel and inter-office dependencies; test single points of failure.                | Dependency and continuity risk register       |\n| Select continuity strategies     | Specify alternate site/work arrangements, manual workarounds, backup systems, staff succession, alternate suppliers, mutual aid and communication methods.          | Continuity strategy matrix                    |\n| Prepare departmental plans       | Set activation, notification, relocation, service prioritization, resource allocation, external communication, record protection and deactivation procedures.       | Department continuity plans                   |\n| Integrate city-wide arrangements | Resolve cross-department dependencies; coordinate with EOC alternate-site arrangements and emergency response plans; define city-wide priorities.                   | Consolidated PSCP                             |\n| Approve and exercise             | Approve through designated executive route; test realistic disruption scenarios and evaluate service restoration.                                                   | Approved PSCP and exercise report             |\n| Review and update                | Update after exercise, incident, staff/technology/site/supplier changes and periodic review; close corrective actions.                                              | Revision log and corrective-action register   |\n\n## SOP 3-8 \\| Barangay DRRM Plan Review and Quality Assurance\n\n**Figure 3.18**\n\n*SOP 3-8 Process Flow: Barangay DRRM Plan Review and Quality Assurance*\n\n![Figure 18: SOP 3-8 | Barangay DRRM Plan Review and Quality Assurance](/library/ch3v10/fig-18.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\nProcess owner: Barangay plan owner: BDRRMC/Barangay Development Council; City reviewer: CDRRMO. Purpose: Maintain a verified and quality-assured city-wide BDRRMP status system.\n\n**Table 3.10**\n\n*SOP 3-8 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                   |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-08 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                             |\n| Trigger           | Yearly BDRRMP cycle; DILG call for plans; a new plan period                                 |\n| Forms             | Form 3-08 Barangay DRRM Plan Review Status                                                  |\n| IAT indicators    | A3.7                                                                                        |\n| Policy references | RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(6); DILG issuances on BDRRM plans; DILG MC 2025-035 (Listo si KAP)      |\n| Records kept in   | Barangay plan status matrix (85 barangays)                                                  |\n\n| **Step / control point**       | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                               | **Mandatory record / output**                   |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Reconcile barangay master list | CPDO, City Secretary and DILG City LGOO validate official barangay count, names, identifiers and boundaries; record authoritative source.                           | Filed merger instrument and 110-to-85 crosswalk |\n| Call for current plans         | Issue formal request with current DILG/OCD template, quality tool, required approval evidence and submission deadline.                                              | Submission notice and receipt log               |\n| Screen completeness            | Check plan period, approval, risk profile, priority hazards, objectives, projects, budgets, roles, warning, evacuation, inclusion and monitoring.                   | Completeness checklist                          |\n| Conduct quality assessment     | Use the current official Quality Assessment System/Quality Assessment Tool and Listo si KAP guidance where applicable; do not substitute an unapproved local score. | Completed official review tool                  |\n| Return and support revision    | Provide itemized comments, technical assistance and a revision due date; separate mandatory criteria from recommendations.                                          | Review letter and assistance record             |\n| Verify correction and status   | Recheck evidence and record status; do not count submission as approval or quality acceptance.                                                                      | Barangay plan status matrix                     |\n| Consolidate and report         | Identify coverage gaps, recurring deficiencies, resource needs and plans requiring exercise; report to CDRRMC/DILG as applicable.                                   | Consolidated report and action register         |\n| Test and update                | Coordinate scenario-based plan testing and update after material changes or exercises.                                                                              | Exercise record and plan revision log           |\n\n## SOP 3-9 \\| Inclusive Risk Assessment and Ecosystem Monitoring Interface\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO coordinates; sector offices and CENRO/DENR retain technical ownership. Purpose: Embed inclusion and protective ecosystem evidence in risk intelligence and preparedness.\n\n**Table 3.11**\n\n*SOP 3-9 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                   |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-09 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                             |\n| Trigger           | Each CDRA or plan update; yearly monitoring                                                 |\n| Forms             | Form 3-10 Consultation and Inclusion Validation Record; Form 3-11                           |\n| IAT indicators    | A5.3, A5.5, A7.6, C3.1, C3.2                                                                |\n| Policy references | RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 10821; RA 8371; RA 10173; RA 9729 as amended       |\n| Records kept in   | Consultation records; ecosystem asset and indicator register                                |\n\n| **Step / control point**                      | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                        | **Mandatory record / output**            |\n|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| Set participation and data protocol           | Identify relevant sector groups and ecosystem custodians; define accessible consultation, lawful data use and consent where applicable.      | Stakeholder and data protocol            |\n| Collect disaggregated evidence                | Use official and lawfully held datasets; minimize personal data; document missing groups and coverage limitations.                           | Disaggregated dataset and privacy review |\n| Validate lived experience and access barriers | Consult women, PWD organizations, older persons, youth/children through appropriate safeguards, IP representatives and affected barangays.   | Consultation record and issue log        |\n| Assess ecosystem services                     | CENRO/DENR and relevant offices identify watershed, upland forest, mangrove and coastal assets, condition indicators and monitoring method.  | Ecosystem asset and indicator register   |\n| Integrate findings                            | Translate barriers and ecosystem findings into CDRA impact chains, MDA interventions, contingency plans and service continuity arrangements. | Updated CDRA/plan crosswalk              |\n| Monitor and report                            | Review inclusion and ecosystem indicators at approved intervals; record trend, uncertainty and corrective action.                            | Annual monitoring report                 |\n\n**Figure 3.19**\n\n*SOP 3-9 Process Flow: Inclusive Risk Assessment and Ecosystem Monitoring Interface*\n\n![Figure 19: SOP 3-9 | Inclusive Risk Assessment and Ecosystem Monitoring Interface](/library/ch3v10/fig-19.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n# 18. Responsibility Assignment (RACI)\n\nRACI clarifies work allocation; it does not transfer statutory or regulatory authority. The matrix below is a proposed operational assignment and must be validated by office heads and the City Legal Office.\n\n| **Activity**                                  | **CDRRMO**     |     | **CPDO/LDC**               |                            | **Engineering/OBO/Zoning** |                    | **Sector offices / utilities** |                                  | **Barangays**       |                           | **CLO / Mayor / CDRRMC**          |\n|-----------------------------------------------|----------------|-----|----------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------|--------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| Maintain risk information register            | A              | R   | C                          |                            | C                          |                    | R                              | C                                | C                   |                           | I                                 |\n| Acquire and validate hazard source data       | R              |     | C                          |                            | C                          |                    | A                              | R for own technical data         | C                   |                           | I                                 |\n| Prepare CDRA                                  | R              | C   | A                          |                            | C                          |                    | R                              |                                  | C                   |                           | I                                 |\n| Approve/use CDRA in CLUP/CDP                  | C              |     | A                          | R                          | C                          |                    | C                              |                                  | C                   |                           | Statutory approval route          |\n| Apply risk screening to investment proposals  | C              |     | A                          | R through planning process | R                          | C technical review | R proposal owner               |                                  | C                   |                           | Budget/LFC/approving body         |\n| Prepare contingency plans                     | A              | R   | C                          |                            | R                          | C                  | R hazard/sector lead           |                                  | R local plan/action |                           | Approval per local issuance       |\n| Prepare PSCP                                  | R              | C   | C                          |                            | C                          |                    | A                              | R by department for own services | I                   | C where service interface | Mayor/City Administrator approval |\n| Prepare and approve BDRRMP                    | C/support      |     | I                          | C                          | I                          |                    | C                              |                                  | A                   | R under applicable law    | DILG technical guidance           |\n| Monitor ecosystem risk-reduction assets       | C              |     | C                          |                            | I                          |                    | A                              | R <u>CENRO/DENR</u>              | C                   |                           | I                                 |\n| Review plan performance and corrective action | R/consolidates |     | R for planning integration |                            | R for regulatory actions   |                    | R for own actions              |                                  | R for own plans     |                           | CDRRMC oversight                  |\n\n# 19. Performance Monitoring, Records and Corrective Action\n\n| **Performance area**   | **Indicator / evidence**                                                                                                               | **Owner / review**                                           |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk data currency     | Share of priority datasets with complete metadata, current source version and assigned custodian; version register                     | CDRRMO GIS/data custodian; quarterly review                  |\n| Hazard map quality     | Priority hazard maps with signed validation, stated scale/use and publication metadata                                                 | CDRRMO and relevant technical office; annual/material update |\n| CDRA completeness      | Approved CDRA package; hazard coverage; exposure database completeness; MDAs; technical validation; correction of Naval/Biliran labels | CPDO/CDRA TWG; at each update                                |\n| Planning mainstreaming | CDRA MDAs cross-referenced to CLUP/CDP/LCCAP/LDIP/AIP and documented zoning/investment decisions                                       | CPDO/LDC; annual planning cycle                              |\n| Contingency readiness  | Priority hazards with approved plans, current contact/resource annexes and exercise/corrective-action records                          | CDRRMO and hazard leads; annual/exercise cycle               |\n| Service continuity     | Departments with completed essential-function inventories, BIAs, continuity strategies and tested plans                                | Continuity executive; annual review                          |\n| Barangay planning      | Verified barangay denominator; plans received, quality-reviewed, approved, tested and updated                                          | CDRRMO / DILG; quarterly status                              |\n| Inclusion              | Plans with documented consultation and accessible arrangements; unresolved barriers and corrective actions                             | Sector offices / CDRRMO; annual and post-event               |\n| Ecosystem assets       | Priority ecosystem assets with baseline condition, indicator, monitoring report and action owner                                       | CENRO/DENR; annual monitoring                                |\n\nThe CDRRMO shall consolidate evidence but shall not certify another office''s technical or statutory performance without that office''s validation. Each corrective action shall identify the finding, root cause, action, accountable owner, due date, resource implication, verification evidence and closure decision. Repeated non-closure shall be escalated through the CDRRMC and the appropriate executive or planning mechanism.\n\n**Figure 3.20**\n\n*Traceability, Records and Corrective Action*\n\n![Figure 20: 19. Performance Monitoring, Records and Corrective Action](/library/ch3v10/fig-20.webp)\n\n*Note.* Performance areas and owners are listed in Section 19.\n\n# 20. Priority Implementation and Validation Agenda\n\n| **Priority**                        | **Required action**                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | **Lead / supporting offices**                                     | **Evidence of completion**                                   |\n|-------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| P1 – Data and methodology integrity | Obtain signed DHSUD MC 2025-004 and current CDRA tools; confirm with OCD Region VIII that the CP Guidebook (2020) and the PSCP Guidebook are the current editions; reconcile IAT version metadata and workbook score/status conflicts | CDRRMO; CPDO; CLO; OCD VIII; DHSUD as needed                      | Controlled source register; resolved version and scoring log |\n| P1 – CDRA completion                | Correct Naval/Biliran labels; validate existing flood/landslide/storm-surge data; scope and complete earthquake, liquefaction, tsunami and other priority hazard assessments as justified                                             | CPDO/CDRA TWG; CDRRMO; PHIVOLCS; MGB; PAGASA; sector offices      | Validated CDRA package and MDA register                      |\n| P1 – Barangay denominator           | Use 85 (PSA PSGC) as the working figure until CPDO, the City Secretary and the DILG City LGOO reconcile the City master list; file the merger instrument and build the 110-to-85 crosswalk for older datasets                         | CPDO; City Secretary; DILG City LGOO                              | Signed barangay master list                                  |\n| P1 – PSCP                           | Designate continuity executive; inventory essential functions; complete BIA and dependencies; prepare, approve and test PSCP                                                                                                          | Mayor/City Administrator; all departments; HRMO; ICT; CDRRMO      | Approved PSCP, BIA and exercise report                       |\n| P1 – Contingency plans              | Confirm priority hazard scenarios; complete missing hazard-specific plans; exercise and close corrective actions                                                                                                                      | CDRRMO; hazard technical agencies; IMT/sector leads; barangays    | Approved plans and exercise records                          |\n| P2 – Planning mainstreaming         | Create CDRA-to-CLUP/CDP crosswalk and DRR/CCA investment screening process                                                                                                                                                            | CPDO/LDC; Budget/LFC; Engineering/OBO/Zoning; CDRRMO              | Adopted screening form and documented planning minutes       |\n| P2 – Critical dependencies          | Convert existing facility/lifeline inventory into validated dependency maps and failure-chain scenarios                                                                                                                               | Asset owners; utilities; CPDO; CDRRMO                             | Validated dependency register and priority actions           |\n| P2 – Inclusion and ecosystems       | Hold documented sector validation; establish ecosystem baseline/indicators and annual monitoring                                                                                                                                      | CSWDO; GAD; PWD/older-person/youth/IP representatives; CENRO/DENR | Consultation evidence and monitoring register                |\n\n# 21. Conclusion\n\nChapter 3 establishes a risk-intelligence and preparedness system that connects authoritative hazard information, method-compliant CDRA, exposure and vulnerability analysis, critical infrastructure dependencies, development planning, contingency planning, service continuity and barangay preparedness. Its central control is traceability: every important risk finding must have a source, a technical owner, a decision use, a responsible office and a record showing whether the required action was completed.\n\nThe chapter is not considered implemented merely because it is approved or included in the Manual of Operations. Institutionalization requires current datasets, signed technical validation, adopted plans, completed screening records, exercised contingency and continuity arrangements, verified barangay coverage, documented corrective actions and periodic evidence-based review.\n\n# REFERENCES\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office. (2026). *CDRRMO reference: Local policies* [Shared Drive folder of the City''s DRRM policy instruments: Sangguniang Panlungsod ordinances, executive orders, CDRRMC resolutions and SP resolutions]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/12NWwUTAE6ub1vW31M2NSMktYlP-BP4oR\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, Sangguniang Panlungsod. (1993–2025). *SP ordinances* [Shared Drive folder, by year, including Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 establishing the CDRRMO and Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015 amending it]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/132g-VeSpXkorcNCte2fpyVY3Az3baBXS\n\n- City Government of Ormoc. (n.d.). Climate and disaster risk assessment (CDRA) final report. City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc. (2017). Comprehensive Land Use Plan 2017–2027 (Volumes 1–3). City Planning and Development Office.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc. (2023). Ormoc City DRRM ten-year plan 2023–2032. City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office.\n\n- Department of Human Settlements and Urban Development. (2025). Memorandum Circular No. 2025-004: Enhanced Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA) Policy. DHSUD.\n\n- Department of Human Settlements and Urban Development. (n.d.). Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment: The 5-Step+ CDRA process. DHSUD Learning Development Repository. https://sites.google.com/dhsud.gov.ph/cdra/introduction-to-cdra/5-steps-cdra-process\n\n- Department of the Interior and Local Government. (2025). Memorandum Circular No. 2025-035: Listo si KAP. DILG.\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council & Office of Civil Defense. (n.d.). Public service continuity planning guidebook. Office of Civil Defense.\n\n- Republic Act No. 7160. (1991). Local Government Code of 1991.\n\n- Republic Act No. 9710. (2009). Magna Carta of Women.\n\n- Republic Act No. 9729. (2009). Climate Change Act of 2009, as amended by Republic Act No. 10174.\n\n- Republic Act No. 10121. (2010). Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010. https://www.lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2010/ra_10121_2010.html\n\n- Republic Act No. 10173. (2012). Data Privacy Act of 2012.\n\n- Republic Act No. 10821. (2016). Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act.\n\n- Republic Act No. 12076. (2024). Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2017). Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities: Detailed Level Assessment.\n\nProject source documents used: Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Chapter 1 Consolidated (October 2026 working draft); Chapter 2 Enhanced Integrated Draft; Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.3 workbook (nine sheets). The workbook''s Guide/Crosswalk version references v1.1 in places; the instrument version history requires reconciliation before the assessment is treated as a controlled baseline.\n\n**Sources (verified 8 November 2026):** The CDRA report, the CLUP 2017–2027, the DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 and the PSCP Guidebook were reviewed for this version (Table 3.1). The NDRRMC–OCD Contingency Planning Guidebook (as of February 2020) was supplied on 8 November 2026 and applied to SOP 3-6. One gap remains: the CDRA copy lacks the original barangay-level risk tables of Chapters 5 and 6. The City shall attach and verify them before final technical adoption.\n'
